<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <application>
        <!--
          Internal payment Activity — launched only via UQPay.createPaymentLauncher.
          Never exported.

          launchMode is deliberately the default (standard), and this used to be singleTop.
          The intent behind singleTop was "a re-launch should reuse the running instance
          instead of stacking a second live payment", and it does — but reuse skips onCreate
          entirely and delivers the new Intent to onNewIntent. With no onNewIntent handler the
          second launch's parameters were simply discarded: the customer went on paying the
          FIRST payment intent, while the merchant's second launch() sat waiting for a result
          that could only ever describe the first one. Launch pi_A, launch pi_B, and pi_A's
          outcome is what pi_B's callback is answered with — the wrong order marked paid.
          That contradicted UQPayPaymentLauncher.launch's own contract ("each call starts a
          fresh payment") and AC §7.1.

          Under standard, each launch gets its own instance, its own launch parameters and its
          own result — one delivery per launch, never a crossed one. What made stacking unsafe
          was never the stacking: it was two Activities sharing one PaymentSession, where the
          first to be destroyed retired the scope out from under the second. PaymentSession now
          counts its hosts and ends the session when the last one leaves, so two instances of
          the same payment share one engine, one confirm and one idempotency pin. A second
          launch of the same intent therefore cannot become a second payment — the guarantee
          singleTop was reached for, now held by the layer that owns it.

          UQPayPaymentActivity.onNewIntent is kept as a backstop for anyone who reintroduces an
          instance-reusing launch mode or an Intent flag.

          configChanges is deliberately narrow and is belt-and-braces, not the fix for B1.
          Handling rotation here keeps the 3-D Secure WebView itself alive, so the issuer's
          challenge page is not even re-fetched when the customer turns the phone — the best
          outcome for someone half-way through typing an OTP. What makes rotation *safe* is
          that the ACS session cookie is no longer deleted with the view (ThreeDsBrowsingState)
          and that the engine is re-attached rather than rebuilt (PaymentSession.obtain); both
          still hold for the config changes not listed here, for process death, and for a host
          that calls recreate().

          Only the four rotation/size qualifiers are claimed. uiMode, locale and fontScale are
          NOT: the sheet must still be recreated for dark mode, a language change and a font
          size change, because that is how it adapts to them.

          android:label names the payment screen in the Recents switcher and in the
          accessibility announcement made when the task comes forward. Without it Android
          falls back to the host application's label, so a customer flicking through Recents
          mid-payment saw the merchant's app name on a screen that is not the merchant's app.

          android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize" is what keeps the Pay button reachable.
          The card form is a scrolling column with the button below it; under the default
          (`adjustPan` on most OEM configurations) the IME simply covers the bottom of the
          window and, on a short screen, the customer cannot scroll to the button that
          submits the payment. adjustResize gives the window the space above the keyboard and
          the existing scroll does the rest.

          android:theme is the SDK's own — see res/values/themes.xml. It replaced
          Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.NoActionBar, which was the only reason androidx.appcompat
          was ever a dependency of this SDK, and whose DayNight behaviour ignored a host app
          that forces its own light or dark mode. That decision now belongs to
          UQPayAppearance.colorMode.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".ui.UQPayPaymentActivity"
            android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|screenLayout|keyboardHidden"
            android:exported="false"
            android:label="@string/uqpay_title"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.UQPay.Payment"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize" />
    </application>

</manifest>
